------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

open question "If the TVC 6th-order filter diverges (accumulator overflow in the filter nodes), what is the observable behaviour? The source code does not appear to include overflow protection within FWDFLTR itself. Overflow in ones-complement arithmetic wraps silently. A divergent filter would produce wild actuator commands, potentially hitting the ACTLIM output limiter, but the limiter only clamps the final output — intermediate node values could still be corrupted. Whether the filter can self-recover after a transient overflow, or whether it would latch into a saturated state, is unclear."

open question "What is the worst-case actuator latency between a body-axis attitude error arising and the corrective actuator command reaching the SPS gimbal servo? The signal path is: CDU read -> rate derivation -> error integration -> 6th-order filter -> output limiter -> optics error counter -> analog servo. The digital portion runs within one T5 period (~50ms per axis), but the analog servo response time and the optics error counter D/A conversion rate add further delay. The total latency budget is relevant for stability margins but is not documented in the AGC source."

-- Resolved: DAP mode-switch has a real but mitigated race window
--
-- RCSDAPON (Comanche055/P40-P47.agc line 2409) updates T5LOC to
-- point to RCSATT before updating FLAGWRD6 mode bits. The comment
-- states "RCSDAPON ENTRY MUST BE UNDER INT-INHIBIT" but the actual
-- caller (DOTVCRCS at line 663) does not execute INHINT before
-- calling RCSDAPON via IBNKCALL.
--
-- The mitigation is the 0.6-second scheduling delay: TIME5 is set
-- to 0.6SECT5, giving TVC T5 tasks time to wind down before the
-- RCS DAP's first T5 fires.
--
-- Worst case: one spurious gimbal command (single actuation pulse)
-- if a stale TVC T5 task fires between the T5LOC update and the
-- FLAGWRD6 update. The window is approximately 4-5 instructions.
-- Practically negligible due to the 0.6-second scheduling delay,
-- but the code does not match its own stated requirements.
